The document was too long to read directly, so here is a detailed summary of all sections:

The paper "Human Capital and Development Accounting: New Evidence from Wage Gains at Migration" by Lutz Hendricks and Todd Schoellman investigates the role of human capital in explaining cross-country income differences utilizing wage data from U.S. immigrants before and after migration. By examining the wage increases immigrants experience when moving to the U.S., the study finds that these gains are less than half of the gap in GDP per worker, suggesting that human capital plays a significant role in income disparities between countries.

Key methodologies include analyzing detailed pre- and post-migration wage data from the New Immigrant Survey (NIS) and additional data from the Mexican Migration Project and the Latin American Migration Project. This approach helps control for the selection bias of immigrants and examines the transferability of skills across countries.

The study identifies that wage gains at migration are approximately 38% of the gap in GDP per worker for immigrants from poorer countries, leading to the inference that human capital accounts for about 62% of cross-country income differences. The paper also shows that wage gains diminish with higher education levels, implying imperfect substitution between skill types in the U.S. labor market.

Conclusions suggest that while human capital accounts for a major share, roughly 60%, of income differences, labor market imperfections and skill transferability issues may cause overestimations. Adjustments for these factors suggest the human capital share could be closer to half. This work, therefore, highlights the need to consider selection bias and imperfect skill applicability in migration-based analyses of development accounting.

